A scanout ring (2ksbox, track M26): a registry through which a display
device whose frames are rendered on the host's GPU (d3dpt-vga's
Direct3D executor) asks the front end for a few images on that GPU,
fills them there, and shows one by number, instead of reading each
frame back into its VRAM for the front end to copy and upload again
(33 MB a frame at 3840x2160). The embed library registers the ops; a
plain qemu-system registers none and every device keeps its surface.
Applies after patch 096, the other ui/console.c one.
Never upstream: the ops are the embed library's contract.
A screendump's update raises qemu_console_pixels_wanted(), so a device
showing a slot puts the frame on its surface first. `held` names the
slots the front end may still be sampling, which the device's next
frame must not go into.

